# {{.Name}}

A [Dygo](https://{{.Framework}}) application.

```sh
cp .env.example .env     # after a fresh clone: .env is git ignored, dygo new made yours
dygo start               # http://localhost:9900, API docs at /docs
dygo start -watch        # restart on every change
go test ./...
dygo check               # validate the module wiring without starting
go build -o app .        # build the binary
docker build -t app .    # build the image
dygo routes              # the route table
dygo g entity User       # users.go, asks for the fields
dygo g crud User         # users.module.go, users.service.go, users.controller.go and a test
```

Everything is in package `main`. `dygo g` writes flat files named after the feature and their role
(`users.service.go` with `UsersService`), so the project stays one folder.

Configuration comes from the environment and `.env` (see `.env.example` and `config.go`).
